findings.body:
The PR description and docs say this endpoint requires `TEAM_ADMIN`, but the handler is decorated with `TEAM_MEMBER`:

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="32-40"}

::callout{type="security"}
Any regular member can mint a week-long invite link and use it to add arbitrary people to the team. For org sub-teams, that can also pull them into the organization. Every other mutating team endpoint, such as memberships and event-types, uses `TEAM_ADMIN`.

The e2e test `should fail to create invite as non-admin member` expects a 403, so it would fail against this code.

::suggestion{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="33-33"}
  @Roles("TEAM_ADMIN")

`createInvite` now returns `newToken.identifier` as `token`:

::diff{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="85-101"}

`identifier` is the constant string `invite-link-for-teamId-<id>`, not the random hex token, while `inviteLink` is still built from the real `token`. So:

- API consumers get a `token` they can't redeem anywhere.
- The response is predictable and identical on every call. The e2e test `should create a new invite on each request` (tokens must differ) and the `inviteLink toContain token` assertion would both fail.
- The tRPC `createInvite.handler.ts` returns this same value to the web UI, so this is a regression for existing callers.

